vironmental strategies to innovate  create value and build so he'll bring it  i think a tremendous perspective to our  panel professor Zhang Jie Liang is one  of the leading economists in China  highly influential in one of the most  highly influential places on the planet  working on climate and energy policy I'm  hope I'm not underestimated I  underestimated in three years ten years  no but he is so he's worth listening to  and he's professor of economics and  executive director of the Institute of  energy environment and economy at  shanghai university in beijing he's he's  authored and led many studies important  studies and initiatives in china and was  a lead author in the last two IPCC  climate change project so welcome so  it's a privilege to have you and thank  you for traveling this great distance  dan did not need to travel a great  distance another great traveler is is  our next panelist is Karen Hussey is an  associate professor at the center school  of environment and society at australian  national university and an expert on  what is now being called the water  energy climate nexus which also i  thought that was great Karen that Neil  also raised that important point in his  introduction she has a book coming out  later this year on  that topic specifically so we'll look  forward to its publication next yes and  buying that it'll be from Cambridge  University Press and she's the author of  environment and sustainability a policy  handbook so welcome and thank you again  for traveling and chill provide us also  some unique perspectives and Luke Tony  shell is a highly respected senior  vehicle analyst for the National Natural  Resources Defense Council he's based in  New York I note that today Luke walked  here which is good than well done Luke  and where he studies ways to reduce the  environment environmental impacts of the  world's transportation system and again  you can think of that impact as you  looked at one of neil's chart spit  specifically on what will happen with  liquid transport fuels and what may  change that to time he also served in  the US navy so that brought him a lot of  perspective on operations and propulsion  as an officer on a navy cruiser so  welcome Luke now in our panel  everybody's been given kind of strict  instructions we'll see how we do to stay  within somewhere between five and ten  minutes and and for their opening  remarks that'll give us a useful  perspective and then we'll open the  discussion to the panelists for  discussion I'll ask some questions the  panelists you're welcome to ask each  other questions and most importantly we  welcome questions from the room so we're  going to leave ample time I think for a  good and open discussion and with that I  think I'll turn over to you Dan to start  off speed up our movement from  presentations and opening comments to  conversation I'll stay seated and I do  think the conference that's been  organized here focuses on a critical  issue at a critical moment which is the  world is changing very fast a lot of the  assumptions we had about what the world  would look like in the years ahead have  been radically shifted in the last few  years three four years  so the world going out to 2030 looks  very different today from what people  asked the same question would have said  at the turn of the century for app for  example 15 years ago obviously the  biggest piece of that in some regards is  the emergence of shale gas tight oil and  the real revolution in the potential for  fossil fuels continuing to play a  central role in our energy future for a  much longer time that some people had  thought but I think at the same time we  see mounting pressure despite policy  failures particularly in Washington and  to some degree internationally on the  issue of climate change and a growing  recognition that that pressure has to be  responded to and will be responded to  one way or another and having just come  from the experience of working in 